  The nethack scoring system works just fine at the low to mid 
player skill levels.  It gives players with no realistic hope
of ascending something to shoot for.  

  High level players count ascension as the most important thing.
Score is secondary them anyway.

  Expert level players look at conducts kept while ascending.

  Wizard level players show off by reaching the maximum possible 
score, playing extinctionist, ascending without eating, ascending 
while keeping difficult combinations of conducts, or ascending EVERY
BLEEDIN' CHARACTER THEY PLAY! Etc., etc.  Flash bastards, the lot of
'em.  ;^D


  Right now the score system only matters to lower level players.
Getting it to accurately reflect skills of higher level players
would be tedious and pointless considering that conducts kept can do
that.  (Note that the devteam doesn't include conducts in the 
score.)


  IMHO this is why #quitting can give you a slightly higher score 
than waiting one more turn to die and why #quitters are recorded in 
the logfile.  It's to encourage lower level players to learn to 
recognize when they're about to die.  Probably the most important 
skill in Nethack.

  So, with the thought in mind that the scoring system is to 
encourage lower level players develop their playing skills[0],
let's look at tweaking the scoring system.  


  Assuming ascension is the goal of every player who hasn't 
ascended yet:


  - Any ascension is better than any non-ascension.  Reflecting
this directly in the score is more trouble than it's worth,
so break the scores display into top ascenders / top non-ascenders.


  Let's continue in this vein.   What's next most important after
ascending?  For the purposes of progress toward ascension not ending
the game by dying is next most important.

  - Any game that didn't end with the player dying counts more than
a game that ends by character death.  In the worst case, a game that
ends by #quitting on turn 1 ranks higher than eit_brad's max scoring
game that ended in death.  This is right and proper in encouraging
unascended players to improve their playing skills.  (No scoring 
system could accurately reflect the importance of eit_brad's 
achievement.  That was the point.)

  To cut a long story short, herewith ranking of games by 
game-ending.  Score to determining ranking within individual classes
of game endings:


   - Ascensions
     - at least one conduct kept
     - no conducts kept
   - Non-ascensions
     - Not ended by death
       - Escaped in celestial disgrace
       - Escaped the dungeon
       - Quitting
     - Ended by death
       - Died on the Astral Plane
       - Died on the Plane of Water
       - Died on the Plane of Fire
       - Died on the Plane of Air
       - Died on the Plane of Earth
       - Died after performing the invocation ritual

  Summary:  

    Taken as given: 
    - A scoring system capable of accurately reflecting the 
      skills of top level players is not worth the trouble.  
    - The current scoring system adequately encourages low-level 
      players toward improving their skills.  
    - If tweaking of game rankings is still desired, then - 
      - The goal of non-ascended players is to ascend.
        - Classify completed games by undeniable progress toward
          ascension.
        - The most important skill for a low level Nethack player 
          is recognizing when death is imminent.
          - Rank any game ended by player death below any game not
            ended by player death.
          - Rank any game ended by quitting ahead of any game ended
            by player death, and below any game ended by escaping.




[0] Top level players can and will decide for themselves what they
think is "best".  A scoring system that includes conducts in some
form will just invite endless debate about the worth of various
conducts.  So leave them out of the scoring system and let people
decide for themselves.



